<<"response-init">>=
##  Cook, E. R. and Kairiukstis, L. A. (1990) Methods of
##  Dendrochronology: Applications in the Environmental Sciences.
##  Cook, E. R. and Peters, K. (1981) The smoothing spline: a new
##  approach to standardizing forest interior tree-ring width series
##  for dendroclimatic studies.

## Smoothing parameter p as a function of the period (nyrs) at which a
## frequency response of f is desired.  This is equation (2) below,
## and it is what the Fortran behind caps() computes.
pCook <- function(nyrs, f = 0.5) {
    6 * f * (cos(2 * pi / nyrs) - 1)^2 / ((1 - f) * (cos(2 * pi / nyrs) + 2))
}

## Frequency response according to Cook and Kairiukstis (citing Cook
## and Peters).  This is equation (1) below.
respCook <- function(f, p) {
    pif2 <- 2 * pi * f
    1 - 1 / (1 + (p * (cos(pif2) + 2)) / (6 * (cos(pif2) - 1)^2))
}
@

\section{Introduction}

This document presents mathematical details about the Dendrochronology
Program Library in R (dplR)~\cite{Bunn2008115,Bunn2010251} which is an
add-on package for R~\cite{Rman}.  Section~\ref{sec:caps} deals with
the spline smoothing function \texttt{caps} whereas
section~\ref{sec:gini.coef} covers the computation of Gini
coefficients in \texttt{gini.coef}.

The original implementations of the functions covered here were not
written by the author of this document.  Therefore the functions were
analyzed with a reverse engineering approach.

This document was first written when spline smoothing in dplR was
performed by \texttt{ffcsaps}, a pure R function.  As of dplR version
1.7.3 that role belongs to \texttt{caps}, a wrapper around a Fortran
subroutine from Ed Cook's ARSTAN, and \texttt{ffcsaps} is deprecated.
The two functions parameterize the spline differently but, as
section~\ref{sec:equivalence} demonstrates, they compute the same
spline.  The analysis below has been rewritten around \texttt{caps},
with the \texttt{ffcsaps} parameterization retained in
section~\ref{sec:ffcsaps-note} because it explains a factor of two
that a reader comparing the two implementations will otherwise trip
over.

\section{Spline Smoothing Parameters in caps}
\label{sec:caps}

The \texttt{caps} function fits a cubic smoothing spline to a given
data vector.  In the manual (Rd file) of the function~\cite{dplRman},
it is stated that the frequency response of the spline is \texttt{f}
at a wavelength (period) of \texttt{nyrs} years\footnote{assuming that
  the sampling rate is once per year}, where these two are parameters
of the function.  We aim to clarify how they relate to the single
smoothing parameter of the spline and what that parameter stands for.

The manual of the \texttt{caps} function cites~\cite{cook1990methods}.
On page 111, they give the following frequency (amplitude) response
function for the spline:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:response}
u(f)=1-\frac{1}{1 + \frac{p(\cos (2\pi f) +2)}{6(\cos (2\pi f) -1)^2}}
\quad ,
\end{equation}
where \(f\) is frequency and \(p\) is stated to be the Lagrange
multiplier of the spline, the single parameter that determines the
frequency response.  However, the exact definition of the optimization
problem is absent.  Neither is it given in~\cite{cook1981smoothing},
the reference used by~\cite{cook1990methods}.  I did not find a copy
of~\cite{peters1981cubic} when trying to follow the chain of
references further.

Note that the relationship between frequency and period using mixed
notation of \texttt{caps} and~\eqref{eq:response} is \(f =
1/\mathtt{nyrs}\).  Setting parameters \texttt{f} and \texttt{nyrs} in
\texttt{caps} is equivalent to the following directive: set the
smoothing parameter to a value that fulfills \(u(1/\mathtt{nyrs}) =
\mathtt{f}\).  By making the variable substitutions and
rearranging~\eqref{eq:response} we get the following equation for
\(p\):
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:p.book}
p = \frac{6 \mathtt{f} (\cos (2\pi / \mathtt{nyrs}) -1)^2}{(1 -
  \mathtt{f})(\cos (2\pi / \mathtt{nyrs}) +2)} \quad .
\end{equation}

\subsection{What the code computes}
\label{sec:caps-code}

The Fortran subroutine called by \texttt{caps} (\texttt{caps\_f} in
\texttt{src/capsf.f95}) sets its smoothing parameter with the
following line, where \texttt{pct} is \texttt{f} and \texttt{v} is
\texttt{nyrs}:
\begin{verbatim}
p=((1.d0/(1.d0-pct)-1.d0)*6.d0*(cos(pi*2.d0/v)-1.d0)**2)
                                       /(cos(pi*2.d0/v)+2.d0)
\end{verbatim}
Since
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:fovers}
\frac{1}{1 - \mathtt{f}} - 1 = \frac{\mathtt{f}}{1 - \mathtt{f}}
\quad ,
\end{equation}
that line is exactly~\eqref{eq:p.book}.  In other words,
\texttt{caps} uses the Lagrange multiplier of~\cite{cook1990methods}
directly, with no reparameterization.  This is a pleasant state of
affairs: the quantity named \texttt{p} in the source code and the
quantity named \(p\) in the book are the same number.  As
section~\ref{sec:ffcsaps-note} describes, that was not true of the
\texttt{ffcsaps} implementation that \texttt{caps} replaced.

\subsection{Empirical frequency response}
\label{sec:empirical}

Whether the fitted spline actually has the advertised frequency
response is a separate question from whether the code implements
\eqref{eq:p.book} correctly, and it is worth checking.  We smooth
\Sexpr{500} independent series of \Sexpr{1536} i.i.d.\ standard normal
samples, take the ratio of the modulus of the discrete Fourier
transform of the smoothed series to that of the input, and average
over the repeats.

<<"response-comp", message=FALSE>>=
N <- 1536
K <- 500
NYRS <- c(4, 16, 64)
nFreq <- N / 2 + 1
halfseq <- seq_len(nFreq)

ratio1 <- array(NA_real_, c(nFreq, K, length(NYRS)))

if (!exists(".Random.seed", globalenv(), mode = "numeric")) {
    foo <- sample(TRUE)
}
seed <- get(".Random.seed", globalenv())
set.seed(123)

for (k in seq_len(K)) {
    x <- rnorm(N)
    fftx <- abs(fft(x))[halfseq]
    for (j in seq_along(NYRS)) {
        fft1 <- abs(fft(caps(x, nyrs = NYRS[j], f = 0.5)))[halfseq]
        ratio1[, k, j] <- fft1 / fftx
    }
}

assign(".Random.seed", seed, globalenv())

response1 <- matrix(NA_real_, nFreq, length(NYRS))
colnames(response1) <- NYRS
for (j in seq_along(NYRS)) {
    response1[, j] <- rowMeans(ratio1[, , j])
}
fftFreq <- seq(from = 0, to = 0.5, length.out = nFreq)

## Simulated response at the nominal cutoff frequency
atCutoff <- vapply(seq_along(NYRS),
                   function(j) approx(fftFreq, response1[, j],
                                      xout = 1 / NYRS[j])$y,
                   numeric(1))
@

<<"caps-caption", cache=FALSE>>=
CAPS_CAPTION <-
    paste("Theoretical frequency response of the spline filter",
          "(equations 1 and 2, green line) versus the response measured",
          "with i.i.d. normal series of 1536 samples, mean of 500",
          "repeats, using \\texttt{caps} (blue circles).  The legend on",
          "the bottom panel applies to all panels.")
@
<<"response", opts.label="myfigures", fig.width=PAGE_WIDTH, fig.height=PAGE_HEIGHT-0.95, fig.cap=CAPS_CAPTION>>=
op <- par(mfcol = c(3, 1), mgp = c(2, 0.75, 0), mar = par("mar") - 1)
LWD <- 3
PCH_1 <- 1
## The simulation has one value per Fourier frequency, which is far too
## dense to plot as points.  Show every SUBth one so that the theoretical
## curve underneath stays visible.
SUB <- seq(from = 1, to = nFreq, by = 10)
for (j in seq_along(NYRS)) {
    plot(fftFreq, response1[, j], type = "n", ylim = c(0, 1),
         xlab = "Frequency (1 / year)", ylab = "Amplitude response",
         main = sprintf("nyrs = %d, f = 0.5", NYRS[j]))
    lines(fftFreq, respCook(fftFreq, pCook(NYRS[j])), col = COLOR_LINE,
          lwd = LWD)
    points(fftFreq[SUB], response1[SUB, j], pch = PCH_1, col = COLOR_SIM,
           cex = 0.8, lwd = 1.5)
    abline(h = 0.5, lty = "dashed")
    abline(v = 1 / NYRS[j], lty = "dashed")
    text(0.35, 0.5, "50% response", pos = 1, offset = 1)
    text(1 / NYRS[j], 0.6, sprintf("%d yr period", NYRS[j]),
         pos = 4, srt = 90, offset = 1)
}
legend("topright", bg = "white",
       legend = c("Simulation (caps)", "Theoretical (Cook and Peters)"),
       col = c(COLOR_SIM, COLOR_LINE),
       lty = c(NA, "solid"), pch = c(PCH_1, NA), lwd = c(1, LWD))
par(op)
@

Figure~\ref{fig:response} shows the result.  Theory meets practice
well, particularly for low frequencies.  The measured response at the
nominal cutoff frequency \(1/\mathtt{nyrs}\) is
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", atCutoff[1])},
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", atCutoff[2])} and
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", atCutoff[3])} for \(\mathtt{nyrs} =
\Sexpr{NYRS[1]}\), \Sexpr{NYRS[2]} and \Sexpr{NYRS[3]} respectively,
against a nominal \(\mathtt{f} = 0.5\).  It must be noted that the
theoretical result does not take into account the effect of having a
series of finite length, which is why the agreement degrades as
\texttt{nyrs} grows toward the length of the series.

\section{Equivalence of caps and the legacy ffcsaps}
\label{sec:equivalence}

Users with results produced by dplR 1.7.2 or earlier will want to know
whether \texttt{caps} changed any numbers.  It did not, with one
documented exception.  Below, the pure R implementation of
\texttt{ffcsaps} as it stood in dplR 1.6.9 is reproduced as
\texttt{ffcsaps.legacy} and compared against \texttt{caps}.

<<"ffcsaps-legacy", echo=FALSE>>=
## Helper used by ffcsaps.legacy()
inc <- function(from, to) {
    if (is.numeric(to) && is.numeric(from) && to >= from) {
        seq(from = from, to = to)
    } else {
        integer(length = 0)
    }
}

## The pure R implementation of ffcsaps() as it stood in dplR 1.6.9,
## before caps() replaced it.  Reproduced here only so that the two
## implementations can be compared; use caps() for real work.
ffcsaps.legacy <- function(y, x = seq_along(y), nyrs = length(y)/2, f = 0.5) {
    ffppual <- function(breaks, c1, c2, c3, c4, x, left) {
        if (left) {
            ix <- order(x)
            x2 <- x[ix]
        } else {
            x2 <- x
        }
        n.breaks <- length(breaks)
        if (left) {
            index <- pmax(ffsorted(breaks[-n.breaks], x2), 1)
        } else {
            index <- ffsorted2(breaks[-1], x2)
        }
        x2 <- x2 - breaks[index]
        v <- x2 * (x2 * (x2 * c1[index] + c2[index]) + c3[index]) + c4[index]
        if (left) v[ix] <- v
        v
    }
    ffsorted <- function(meshsites, sites) {
        index <- order(c(meshsites, sites))
        which(index > length(meshsites)) - seq_along(sites)
    }
    ffsorted2 <- function(meshsites, sites) {
        index <- order(c(sites, meshsites))
        which(index <= length(sites)) - seq(from = 0, to = length(sites) - 1)
    }
    ## Similar in function to spdiags(B, d, n, n) in MATLAB
    spdiags <- function(B, d, n) {
        n.d <- length(d)
        A <- matrix(0, n.d * n, 3)
        count <- 0
        for (k in seq_len(n.d)) {
            this.diag <- d[k]
            i <- inc(max(1, 1 - this.diag), min(n, n - this.diag))
            n.i <- length(i)
            if (n.i > 0) {
                j <- i + this.diag
                row.idx <- seq(from = count + 1, by = 1, length.out = n.i)
                A[row.idx, 1] <- i
                A[row.idx, 2] <- j
                A[row.idx, 3] <- B[j, k]
                count <- count + n.i
            }
        }
        A <- A[A[, 3] != 0, , drop = FALSE]
        A[order(A[, 2], A[, 1]), , drop = FALSE]
    }
    y2 <- as.numeric(y)
    x2 <- as.numeric(x)
    n <- length(x2)
    if (n < 3) stop("there must be at least 3 data points")
    ix <- order(x2)
    zz1 <- n - 1
    xi <- x2[ix]
    zz2 <- n - 2
    diff.xi <- diff(xi)
    if (any(diff.xi == 0)) stop("the data abscissae must be distinct")
    if (n != length(y2))
        stop("abscissa and ordinate vector must be of the same length")
    arg2 <- -1:1
    odx <- 1 / diff.xi
    R <- spdiags(cbind(c(diff.xi[-c(1, zz1)], 0),
                       2 * (diff.xi[-1] + diff.xi[-zz1]),
                       c(0, diff.xi[-c(1, 2)])), arg2, zz2)
    R2 <- spdiags(cbind(c(odx[-zz1], 0, 0),
                        c(0, -(odx[-1] + odx[-zz1]), 0),
                        c(0, 0, odx[-1])), arg2, n)
    R2[, 1] <- R2[, 1] - 1
    forR <- Matrix(0, zz2, zz2, sparse = TRUE)
    forR2 <- Matrix(0, zz2, n, sparse = TRUE)
    forR[R[, 1:2, drop = FALSE]] <- R[, 3]
    forR2[R2[, 1:2, drop = FALSE]] <- R2[, 3]
    ## This is equation (6): the ffcsaps parameterization
    p.inv <- (1 - f) * (cos(2 * pi / nyrs) + 2) /
        (12 * f * (cos(2 * pi / nyrs) - 1)^2) + 1
    yi <- y2[ix]
    p <- 1 / p.inv
    mplier <- 6 - 6 / p.inv
    u <- as.numeric(solve(mplier * tcrossprod(forR2) + forR * p,
                          diff(diff(yi) / diff.xi)))
    yi <- yi - mplier * diff(c(0, diff(c(0, u, 0)) / diff.xi, 0))
    test0 <- xi[-c(1, n)]
    c3 <- c(0, u / p.inv, 0)
    x3 <- c(test0, seq(from = xi[1], to = xi[n], length = 101))
    cc.1 <- diff(c3) / diff.xi
    cc.2 <- 3 * c3[-n]
    cc.3 <- diff(yi) / diff.xi - diff.xi * (2 * c3[-n] + c3[-1])
    cc.4 <- yi[-n]
    to.sort <- c(test0, x3)
    ix.final <- order(to.sort)
    tmp <- unique(data.frame(
        to.sort[ix.final],
        c(ffppual(xi, cc.1, cc.2, cc.3, cc.4, test0, FALSE),
          ffppual(xi, cc.1, cc.2, cc.3, cc.4, x3, TRUE))[ix.final]))
    tmp2 <- tmp
    tmp2[[1]] <- round(tmp2[[1]], 5)
    res <- tmp2[[2]][tmp2[[1]] %in% x2]
    if (length(res) != n)
        res <- approx(x = tmp[[1]], y = tmp[[2]], xout = x2,
                      ties = "ordered")$y
    res
}
@

<<"equiv-comp", message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>=
if (!exists(".Random.seed", globalenv(), mode = "numeric")) {
    foo <- sample(TRUE)
}
seed <- get(".Random.seed", globalenv())
set.seed(42)

data(ca533)
cam011 <- as.numeric(na.omit(ca533[, "CAM011"]))
cases <- list(
    "i.i.d. normal, n = 200" = rnorm(200, 100, 20),
    "noisy sine wave, n = 100" =
        5 * sin(seq(from = 0, to = 6 * pi, length.out = 101)[-101]) +
        rnorm(100) + 20,
    "AR(1), phi = 0.7, n = 500" =
        as.numeric(arima.sim(list(ar = 0.7), 500)) + 50,
    "ca533 series CAM011" = cam011)

assign(".Random.seed", seed, globalenv())

equivGrid <- expand.grid(case = names(cases), nyrs = c(10, 32),
                         f = c(0.5, 0.9), stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
equivGrid$maxdiff <- vapply(seq_len(nrow(equivGrid)), function(i) {
    y <- cases[[equivGrid$case[i]]]
    max(abs(ffcsaps.legacy(y, nyrs = equivGrid$nyrs[i], f = equivGrid$f[i]) -
            caps(y, nyrs = equivGrid$nyrs[i], f = equivGrid$f[i])))
}, numeric(1))
worstInteger <- max(equivGrid$maxdiff)

## The one place the two differ: caps() coerces nyrs to an integer
fracNyrs <- 2 * length(cam011) / 3
diffFrac <- max(abs(ffcsaps.legacy(cam011, nyrs = fracNyrs, f = 0.5) -
                    caps(cam011, nyrs = fracNyrs, f = 0.5)))
diffTrunc <- max(abs(ffcsaps.legacy(cam011, nyrs = trunc(fracNyrs), f = 0.5) -
                     caps(cam011, nyrs = fracNyrs, f = 0.5)))
camRange <- diff(range(cam011))
@

<<"equiv-table", results="asis">>=
cat("\\begin{table}[tbp]\n\\centering\n")
cat("\\caption{Largest absolute difference between",
    "\\texttt{ffcsaps.legacy} and \\texttt{caps} over all fitted values,",
    "for integer \\texttt{nyrs}.}\n")
cat("\\label{tab:equiv}\n")
cat("\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\n")
cat("Series & \\texttt{nyrs} & \\texttt{f} &",
    "max.\\ abs.\\ difference \\\\\n\\midrule\n")
ord <- order(match(equivGrid$case, names(cases)), equivGrid$nyrs, equivGrid$f)
for (i in ord) {
    cat(sprintf("%s & %d & %.1f & %.2e \\\\\n",
                latexify(equivGrid$case[i], doublebackslash = FALSE),
                equivGrid$nyrs[i], equivGrid$f[i], equivGrid$maxdiff[i]))
}
cat("\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n")
@

Table~\ref{tab:equiv} gives the largest absolute difference between
the two implementations across four test series, two values of
\texttt{nyrs} and two values of \texttt{f}.  The worst case is
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1e", worstInteger)}, which is floating-point noise.
For integer \texttt{nyrs}, \texttt{caps} and \texttt{ffcsaps} compute
the same spline.

There is one genuine difference.  \texttt{caps} passes \texttt{nyrs}
to Fortran as an integer, so a fractional \texttt{nyrs} is truncated,
whereas \texttt{ffcsaps} used it as given.  Fitting series CAM011 of
the \texttt{ca533} data set with \(\mathtt{nyrs} =
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", fracNyrs)}\), two thirds of the series length,
the two differ by \Sexpr{sprintf("%.2e", diffFrac)}, which is
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", 100 * diffFrac / camRange)}\% of the range of
the series.  Passing the truncated value \(\Sexpr{trunc(fracNyrs)}\)
to \texttt{ffcsaps} instead brings the difference back down to
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1e", diffTrunc)}, confirming that truncation is the
whole of the discrepancy.

This is reachable in ordinary use, by two routes.  A \texttt{nyrs}
between 0 and 1 selects the proportion-of-series-length shorthand, and
\texttt{caps} multiplies it by the series length, which will rarely
land on a whole number.  Internally, \texttt{plot.crn},
\texttt{wavelet.plot} and \texttt{ssf} pass a fractional
\texttt{nyrs} of their own, computed as a fixed proportion of the
series length; \texttt{detrend.series} and \texttt{rcs} apply
\texttt{floor} first and so are unaffected.  The effect on the fitted
curve is small, but it is not zero.

<<"equiv-caption", cache=FALSE>>=
EQUIV_CAPTION <-
    paste("Top: series CAM011 of the \\texttt{ca533} data set (grey)",
          "with a 32-year spline fitted by \\texttt{caps} (blue) and by",
          "the legacy \\texttt{ffcsaps} (vermillion, dashed); the two",
          "curves are indistinguishable.  Bottom: the difference",
          "between the two fitted curves, in ring-width units.")
@
<<"equiv-fig", opts.label="myfigures", fig.width=PAGE_WIDTH, fig.height=PAGE_WIDTH*1.1, fig.cap=EQUIV_CAPTION>>=
capsFit <- caps(cam011, nyrs = 32, f = 0.5)
ffFit <- ffcsaps.legacy(cam011, nyrs = 32, f = 0.5)
op <- par(mfcol = c(2, 1), mgp = c(2, 0.75, 0), mar = par("mar") - 1)
plot(cam011, type = "l", col = COLOR_REF,
     xlab = "Index", ylab = "Ring width (mm)",
     main = "CAM011 with a 32-year spline")
lines(capsFit, col = COLOR_SIM, lwd = 3)
lines(ffFit, col = COLOR_ALT, lwd = 2, lty = "dashed")
legend("topright", bty = "n", cex = 0.85,
       legend = c("data", "caps", "ffcsaps (legacy)"),
       col = c(COLOR_REF, COLOR_SIM, COLOR_ALT),
       lty = c("solid", "solid", "dashed"), lwd = c(1, 3, 2))
plot(capsFit - ffFit, type = "l", col = COLOR_SIM,
     xlab = "Index", ylab = "caps - ffcsaps",
     main = "Difference between the two fits")
abline(h = 0, lty = "dashed", col = COLOR_REF)
par(op)
@

Figure~\ref{fig:equiv-fig} shows the two fits on a real ring-width
series together with their difference, which is at the level of the
floating-point representation.

\section{A note on the ffcsaps parameterization}
\label{sec:ffcsaps-note}

The deprecated \texttt{ffcsaps} contained code lines corresponding to
the equation
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:pinv.code}
\mathtt{p.inv} = \frac{1}{\mathtt{p}} = \frac{(1 - \mathtt{f})(\cos
  (2\pi / \mathtt{nyrs}) +2)}{12 \mathtt{f} (\cos (2\pi /
  \mathtt{nyrs}) -1)^2} + 1 \quad ,
\end{equation}
where \(\mathtt{p}\) and its inverse \(\mathtt{p.inv}\) are variables
used in the code.  Writing~\eqref{eq:p.book} for the inverse,
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:pinv.book}
\frac{1}{p} = \frac{(1 - \mathtt{f})(\cos (2\pi / \mathtt{nyrs})
  +2)}{6 \mathtt{f} (\cos (2\pi / \mathtt{nyrs}) -1)^2} \quad ,
\end{equation}
we find that~\eqref{eq:pinv.book} and~\eqref{eq:pinv.code} are
connected by
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:pp.inv}
\frac{1}{p} = 2 \left(\frac{1}{\mathtt{p}} - 1\right)
\end{equation}
or equivalently
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:pp}
\frac{\mathtt{p}}{1 - \mathtt{p}} = 2 p \quad .
\end{equation}
So the variable named \texttt{p} in \texttt{ffcsaps} and the
Lagrange multiplier \(p\) of~\cite{cook1990methods} were not the same
quantity, despite sharing a name.  They are two parameterizations of
the same penalty, related by~\eqref{eq:pp}.  This is the factor of two
that a reader comparing \texttt{src/capsf.f95}
against \texttt{ffcsaps} would otherwise have to discover the hard
way.

The \texttt{ffcsaps} form is the convex-combination parameterization,
in which the spline minimizes
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:sum}
\mathtt{p} \times \text{Error} + (1 - \mathtt{p}) \times
\text{Roughness}
\end{equation}
with \(\mathtt{p} \in [0, 1]\).  Following from~\eqref{eq:pp}
and~\eqref{eq:sum}, the splines described in~\cite{cook1990methods},
and hence those computed by \texttt{caps}, are the result of
minimizing
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:cooksum}
2 p \times \text{Error} + \text{Roughness} \quad ,
\end{equation}
with the same definitions of Error and Roughness, details of which are
omitted here.  Section~\ref{sec:equivalence} confirms empirically that
the two forms give the same fitted curve.

\section{Formulation of Gini Coefficient in gini.coef}
\label{sec:gini.coef}

The \texttt{gini.coef} function computes the Gini coefficient (Gini
index) of a given data vector.  The manual (Rd file) of the function
has a reference to~\cite{biondi2008inequality} which uses the
following formula for the Gini coefficient (\(G\)):
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:rmd}
  G = \frac{1}{2 n \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n}
  \left| x_i - x_j \right| \quad .
\end{equation}
In~\eqref{eq:rmd}, the Gini coefficient is defined in terms of
pairwise differences between all pairs of observations (\(x_i\text{,
}i \in 1, \dots, n\)). More specifically, the Gini coefficient is one
half of the relative mean difference, which is defined as the mean of
the absolute pairwise distances divided by the mean of the
observations.

The C source code of the \texttt{gini.coef} function uses the
following formula for the Gini index:
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:lorenz}
  G = \left(X_n (n - 1) - 2 \sum_{i=1}^{n-1}X_i\right) / (X_n n) \quad ,
\end{equation}
where \(n\) is the number of observations and \(X_i\) is the
\(i\text{:th}\) cumulative sum
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:cumsum}
  X_i = \sum_{j=1}^{i} x_j
\end{equation}
of sorted observations \(x_j\):
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:sorted}
  \forall i: i < j \Rightarrow x_i \leq x_j \quad .
\end{equation}
\eqref{eq:lorenz} can be reformulated as
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:lorenz2}
  G = 1 - \frac{1}{n} - \frac{2}{X_n n} \sum_{i=1}^{n-1}X_i
\end{equation}
or as
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:lorenz3}
  G = \left(\frac{1}{2} - \left(\frac{1}{2n} + \frac{1}{X_n n}
      \sum_{i=1}^{n-1}X_i\right)\right) / \frac{1}{2} \quad .
\end{equation}
When we assign
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:AB}
  A + B = \frac{1}{2}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:B}
  B = B_1 + B_2 = \frac{1}{2n} + \frac{1}{X_n n} \sum_{i=1}^{n-1}X_i
  \quad ,
\end{equation}
\eqref{eq:lorenz3} becomes
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:lorenzsymb}
  G = A / (A + B)
\end{equation}
or equivalently
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:lorenzsymb2}
  G = 1 - 2 B \quad .
\end{equation}

Figure~\ref{fig:lorenz} is a graphical representation of the Gini
coefficient using an example data set of the following six observed
values: \(\{0.2, 0.4, 0.75, 0.95, 1.2, 2.5\}\). It shows the
definition of the Gini coefficient as the ratio of the area above the
Lorenz curve~\cite{lorenz1905} to the total area of the
triangle~\cite{xu2003has}.  The Lorenz curve is defined by the
cumulative distribution function of the empirical probability
distribution of the observations.  The sides of the triangle
corresponding to the axes are normalized to length 1.

Comparing Figure~\ref{fig:lorenz} to \eqref{eq:B}, \(B_2 =
\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}X_i / (X_n n)\) is obviously the sum of the areas of
the \textcolor{cyan}{cyan} bars.  Summing the areas of the
\textcolor{teal}{teal} triangles, we get
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:B1}
\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left( \frac{1}{2} \frac{1}{n} \frac{x_i}{X_n} \right) =
\frac{1}{2 n X_n}\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i = \frac{1}{2 n} = B_1 \quad .
\end{equation}
Note that \(B_1\) only depends on the number of observations, not on
their values.  From~\eqref{eq:B} and~\eqref{eq:lorenzsymb2} we find
that the value of the Gini coefficient at maximum inequality (winner
takes all) is \(G_{\text{max}}(n)=1 - 1 / n\).  When all observed
values are equal, the Lorenz curve matches the line of equality
(Figure~\ref{fig:lorenz}), and the Gini coefficient is
\(G_{\text{min}}=0\).  We have assumed that all values \(x_i\) are
non-negative.

\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}
    % define points
    \coordinate (origin) at (0,0);
    \coordinate (A) at (1,0.2);
    \coordinate (B) at (2,0.6);
    \coordinate (C) at (3,1.35);
    \coordinate (D) at (4,2.3);
    \coordinate (E) at (5,3.5);
    \coordinate (F) at (6,6);
    % fills
    \fill[pink] (origin) -- (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- (E) -- (F);
    \fill[teal] (origin) -- +(1,0) -- (A);
    \fill[teal] (A) -- +(1,0) -- (B);
    \fill[teal] (B) -- +(1,0) -- (C);
    \fill[teal] (C) -- +(1,0) -- (D);
    \fill[teal] (D) -- +(1,0) -- (E);
    \fill[teal] (E) -- +(1,0) -- (F);
    \fill[cyan] (1,0) -- (A) -- +(1,0) -- (B) -- +(1,0) -- (C) --
    +(1,0) -- (D) -- +(1,0) -- (E) -- +(1,0) -- (6,0);
    % triangle
    \draw (0,0) -- (6,0) -- (6,6) -- cycle;
    % x axis ticks
    \foreach \x in {1,2,3,4,5,6} \draw (\x,-1pt) -- (\x,1pt);
    % draw points
    \foreach \coord in {(A),(B),(C),(D),(E),(F)}
    \draw \coord circle [radius=2pt];
    % draw lines
    \draw (origin) -- (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- (E) -- (F);
    \draw (1,0) -- (A) -- +(1,0) -- (B) -- +(1,0) -- (C) -- +(1,0) --
    (D) -- +(1,0) -- (E) -- +(1,0);
    % legend
    \draw (0.5,5.5) node[fill=pink] {}; \draw(1, 5.5) node {\(A\)};
    \draw (0.5,5) node[fill=teal] {};   \draw(1, 5) node {\(B_1\)};
    \draw (0.5,4.5) node[fill=cyan] {}; \draw(1, 4.5) node {\(B_2\)};
    \draw [decoration={brace},decorate] (1.4,5.2) -- (1.4,4.3);
    \draw(1.8, 4.75) node {\(B\)};
    % axis tick labels
    \draw (0,-7pt) node[above=-6pt] {\(0\)};
    \draw (1,-7pt) node {\(1/6\)};
    \draw (2,-7pt) node {\(2/6\)};
    \draw (3,-7pt) node {\(3/6\)};
    \draw (4,-7pt) node {\(4/6\)};
    \draw (5,-7pt) node {\(5/6\)};
    \draw (6,-7pt) node[above=-6pt] {\(1\)};
    \draw (6cm+7pt,0) node[rotate=90] {\(0\)};
    \draw (6cm+7pt,6) node[rotate=90] {\(1\)};
    % axis titles
    \draw (3,-26pt) node[align=center]
    {Cumulative portion of population\\(ordered from lowest to highest
    value)};
    \draw (6cm+22pt,3) node[align=center,rotate=90]
    {Cumulative sum of values\\divided by total};
    % text for Lorenz curve
    \draw (D)+(-7pt,7pt) node[rotate=47] {Lorenz curve};
    % text for Line of equality
    \draw (3,3)+(-7pt,7pt) node[rotate=45] {Line of equality};
  \end{tikzpicture}
  \caption{Graphical representation of the Gini coefficient based on
    areas defined by the Lorenz curve (\(n=6\)).  See
    equations~\eqref{eq:AB}, \eqref{eq:B}, \eqref{eq:lorenzsymb}
    and~\eqref{eq:lorenzsymb2}. }
  \label{fig:lorenz}
\end{figure}

The equivalence of different definitions of the Gini coefficient is
reviewed in~\cite{xu2003has}.  One of the results shown in the paper
is that the geometric definition~\eqref{eq:lorenzsymb} used by the
\texttt{gini.coef} function is equivalent to the definition based on
the relative mean difference~\eqref{eq:rmd}.  This can be
experimentally verified by comparing the results of the following R
function to those of \texttt{gini.coef}.
<<gini-rmd, echo=TRUE, tidy=FALSE>>=
## Gini index is one half of relative mean difference.
## x should not have NA values.
gini.rmd <- function(x) {
    mean(abs(outer(x, x, "-"))) / mean(x) * 0.5
}
@
<<"gini-check", echo=TRUE>>=
giniMax <- max(abs(vapply(ca533, function(x) {
    x <- x[!is.na(x)]
    gini.rmd(x) - gini.coef(x)
}, numeric(1))))
giniMax
@
Over all \Sexpr{ncol(ca533)} series of the \texttt{ca533} data set the
two agree to \Sexpr{sprintf("%.1e", giniMax)}.

